<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ngāi Tahu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kāi Tahu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ngāi Tahu is the principal Māori iwi, or tribe, of the South Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, whose territory covers most of the island. Its claims over land purchases by the Crown in the nineteenth century were pursued for more than a century, and the settlement of 1998 brought a Crown apology and financial redress. Its commercial arm has since become one of the largest businesses in the South Island.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ngai-tahu.jpg" alt="Ngāi Tahu" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Igover · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ng%C4%81i_Tahu_Takiwa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ngāi Tahu means People of Tahu, after the ancestor Tahupōtiki, younger brother of Porourangi and a descendant of Paikea. Tradition holds that he was born at Whāngārā around 1450 and took the Tākitimu canoe to the South Island. The iwi arose in the Gisborne District of the North Island, alongside Ngāti Porou and Ngāti Kahungunu, all intermarrying with the local Ngāti Ira. Its people moved down the coast to the Heretaunga and on towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> before crossing to the South Island.</p><p>There they met iwi who had come earlier: Kāti Māmoe, who had arrived about a century before and conquered Waitaha, as well as Ngāi Tara, Rangitāne, Ngāti Tūmatakōkiri and Ngāti Wairangi. Ngāi Tahu absorbed several of these groups, and most families of Ngāi Tahu descent today also have Ngāti Māmoe ancestry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century, after establishing their dominance down to Kaikōura, Ngāi Tahu chiefs pushed further south. In the 1820s the Kaihuānga feud, sparked when a woman wore a dogskin cloak belonging to the paramount chief Tamaiharanui, showed the force of an ariki's tapu. After Europeans arrived, Ngāti Toa, allied with Ngāti Tama, and Ngāti Rārua invaded Ngāi Tahu territory with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, and in the 19th century European settlers, mostly British, came in their hundreds of thousands.</p><p>Over 270 people of Ngāi Tahu descent served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, some in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> (Māori) Pioneer Battalion. In 2021 the iwi began legal proceedings against the government, claiming that successive governments had failed to protect the waterways and asking the Crown to recognise its rangatiratanga over rivers across most of the South Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The tribal area, or takiwā, is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, running from the White Bluffs and Kahurangi Point in the north to Stewart Island / Rakiura, and comprises 18 rūnanga based on traditional settlements, each with its marae. The five primary hapū are Ngāti Kurī, Ngāti Irakehu, Kāti Huirapa, Ngāi Tūāhuriri and Ngāi Te Ruahikihiki. In the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> 84,969 people affiliated with the iwi. Te Rūnanga o Ngāi Tahu, its governance body, works under the Ngāi Tahu Claims Settlement Act 1998. Many southern Ngāi Tahu once spoke a distinct dialect, sometimes called Southern Māori, and the iwi runs its own radio station, Tahu FM, founded in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ngāi Tahu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty settlement process is among the most extensive attempts by a settler society to address historical injustice: major settlements included the fisheries agreement of 1992, the Waikato-Tainui settlement of 1995 and the <b>Ngāi Tahu</b> settlement of 1998, each accompanied by a Crown apology or redress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
